## Why This Is Asked
Tests an asynchronous, idempotent flow common in payments; exercises end-to-end behavior, including 202 semantics, Location header, polling, and fault tolerance.

## Key Concepts
- Idempotency keys prevent duplicate side effects
- Asynchronous processing and status polling
- Proper error handling for missing/invalid headers
- Downstream failure resilience with preserved idempotency

## Code Example
```javascript
// Jest-like sketch
test('idempotent POST /payments', async () => {
  const key = 'idem-123';
  const r1 = await post('/payments', { amount: 100 }, { 'Idempotency-Key': key });
  const r2 = await post('/payments', { amount: 100 }, { 'Idempotency-Key': key });
  expect(r1.status).toBe(202);
  expect(r1.headers.Location).toContain('/payments/');
  expect(r2.status).toBe(202);
  expect(r2.body.id).toBe(r1.body.id);
  // poll until final state
  const id = r1.body.id;
  let s = await get(`/payments/${id}/status`);
  while (s.state === 'pending') s = await get(`/payments/${id}/status`);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend tests for long-running payments and timeouts?
- How would you test idempotency across multiple regions or microservices?

## Why This Is Asked

